{
  "result": "Every morning, the town\u2019s smallest library opens before the bakery, though almost nobody notices the light appearing in its upstairs window.\n\nIts librarian, Mara, likes that quiet hour because books seem less like objects and more like conversations waiting patiently to resume. She waters a stubborn fern, straightens a chair, and returns yesterday\u2019s discoveries to shelves that have gradually learned their shapes.\n\nAt nine, the first visitors arrive: a retired mechanic reading poetry, two children chasing dragons, and a student avoiding mathematics. Mara knows better than to ask whether anyone needs help immediately; sometimes people need a place before they need an answer.\n\nBy noon, sunlight reaches the back table, where strangers occasionally exchange recommendations and discover they have been neighbors for years. The library has no grand architecture, no famous collection, and a roof that complains whenever the rain grows especially enthusiastic.\n\nYet its modest rooms hold something difficult to measure: the permission to be curious without having to prove curiosity useful. Here, a person can learn about distant planets, borrow a recipe, or simply sit somewhere warm while the afternoon passes.\n\nWhen evening comes, Mara checks the windows and notices which books have vanished into backpacks, coat pockets, and waiting hands. She imagines them traveling through kitchens and bus stations, opening small doors in minds she may never come to know.\n\nThen she switches off the light, leaving the fern, the shelves, and a thousand unfinished conversations to rest until the town wakes up again early tomorrow morning.",
